// Regression (BUG#61b, native build path): `Vec[T].get(i)` — a generic METHOD on a
// generic TYPE returning Option[T] — segfaulted natively. TWO stacked root causes:
//
// 1. Generic-overload mangling collision (fixed 1cc867b): all same-name generic
//    templates mangled to one instance name for equal type args, so a call site
//    could silently reuse a DIFFERENT overload's instantiation (wrong signature/
//    return type). See generic_overload_mangle.ax.
// 2. std/collections.ax was NOT in the native build's hardcoded stdlib bundle
//    (concatenate_stdlib in main_air.ax) — `import std.collections` was silently
//    stripped, struct Vec and the get/push templates didn't exist in the unit, and
//    `v.push` dispatched through garbage with no compile-time diagnostic.
//    Fixed by bundling collections.ax like the other std files.
//
// Still open (BUG#62): HashMap natively hits the explicit-generic-instantiation
// call form `hash_key[K](key)` which resolves to nothing and links against a
// phantom `ax_hash_key` — same class as `new[i32]()`. Vec is unaffected.
import std.collections

fn main() -> i32:
    mut v = Vec[i32].new()
    v.push(3)
    v.push(9)
    let o = v.get(0)
    if o.is_some():
        println("is-some-ok")
    else:
        println("is-some-wrong")
    if o.unwrap() == 3:
        println("unwrap-ok")
    else:
        println("unwrap-wrong")
    let o2 = v.get(1)
    if o2.unwrap() == 9:
        println("unwrap2-ok")
    else:
        println("unwrap2-wrong")
    let o3 = v.get(5)
    if o3.is_some():
        println("oob-wrong")
    else:
        println("oob-ok")
    return 0
